
==============================================================================
S1  Leaf identity of the even->3n+1 arrows; multiples of 3 are transient
==============================================================================
universe: targets v in [1, 100000]; predecessor sets cross-checked
new (even -> 3n+1) arrows landing in [1,100000]: 16666 = #{v = 1 mod 6, 7 <= v <= 100000} = 16666
PROVED (S1.1): for v=6j+1>=7 the unique even 3n+1-predecessor 2j=(v-1)/3 equals
  R^{-1}(n0), n0=(4v-1)/3 the least odd T-predecessor (n0 = 1 mod 8, 3n0+1=4v);
  i.e. E extends fibre (B1) of the inherited note to index j=-1 exactly once.
PROVED (S1.2): targets 5 mod 6 and 3 mod 6 acquire no new predecessor.
PROVED (S1.3): no arrow of E enters 3Z from outside 3Z; inside 3Z only halving
  arrows stay (an acyclic forest), so every multiple of 3 is a transient node
  and a singleton SCC.  Checked n <= 100000.
Sample: predecessors of 7: {14, 2};  of 13: {26, 4};  of 25: {50, 8};  of 5: {10}; of 9: {18}
   v=  7  preds=[2, 14]  new_even_pred=[2]
   v= 13  preds=[4, 26]  new_even_pred=[4]
   v= 25  preds=[8, 50]  new_even_pred=[8]
   v=  5  preds=[10]  new_even_pred=[]
   v=  9  preds=[18]  new_even_pred=[]
   v= 11  preds=[22]  new_even_pred=[]
   v= 19  preds=[6, 38]  new_even_pred=[6]
   v= 31  preds=[10, 62]  new_even_pred=[10]

==============================================================================
S2  Q1/Q2, inverse moves, residue drift, and the 3-adic hostile family
==============================================================================
residue m mod 9 | k_min | admissible k mod 6 | factor 2^k/3 | result mod 3
   1            |  2    | [2, 4]             | 4/3          | 1
   2            |  1    | [1, 3]             | 2/3          | 1
   4            |  0    | [0, 2]             | 1/3          | 1
   5            |  3    | [3, 5]             | 8/3          | 1
   7            |  0    | [0, 4]             | 1/3          | 2
   8            |  1    | [1, 5]             | 2/3          | 2
PROVED (S2.1): k parity = [m = 2 mod 3]; admissible k form two classes mod 6
  (the third class of the right parity gives a result 0 mod 3).
  4,7 mod 9 shrink x1/3; 2,8 shrink x2/3; 1 grows x4/3; 5 grows x8/3.
  From residues {1,2,4,5} the result is 1 mod 3; from {7,8} it is 2 mod 3.

Hostile family m = 3^j + 1 (m = 1 mod 3^j): greedy forces j-1 steps x4/3 then x1/3
   j |      m | greedy values until first shrink | net ratio | 4^(j-1)/3^j | >1 ?
   1 |      4 | [4, 1] | 1/4 | 1/3 | False
   2 |     10 | [10, 13, 4] | 2/5 | 4/9 | False
   3 |     28 | [28, 37, 49, 16] | 4/7 | 16/27 | False
   4 |     82 | [82, 109, 145, 193, 64] | 32/41 | 64/81 | False
   5 |    244 | [244, 325, 433, 577, 769, 256] | 64/61 | 256/243 | True
   6 |    730 | [730, 973, 1297, 1729, 2305, 3073, 1024] | 512/365 | 1024/729 | True
   7 |   2188 | [2188, 2917, 3889, 5185, 6913, 9217, 12289, 4096] | 1024/547 | 4096/2187 | True
   8 |   6562 | [6562, 8749, 11665, 15553, 20737, 27649, 36865, 49153, 16384] | 8192/3281 | 16384/6561 | True
PROVED (S2.2): m = 1 mod 3^j, m != 1 mod 3^(j+1)  =>  greedy k-word starts 2^(j-1) 0 and
  the j-th greedy image is 4^(j-1)(m-1)/3^j < m  iff  j <= 4.  First exceeding family j=5:
  244 -> 325 -> 433 -> 577 -> 769 -> 256 = 2^8 (net 256/244 = 64/61 > 1).
  This is the 3-adic mirror of the 2-adic hostile n = 2^(L+1)-1 (inherited (B5) block).
  greedy from 244 continues: status=below steps=6 peak=2308 final=85

