Conjecture C_E: in E the non-multiples of 3 form ONE strongly connected component and every
  multiple of 3 is a transient singleton feeding it.  Status: Q1 part is Collatz; Q2 part is NEW
  and OPEN; both FINITE-EXACT to 10^6.  The finite SCC underestimates because membership of n in
  the giant SCC of E|[1,N] needs BOTH a forward path n->1 and an inverse path m->1 that stay
  inside [1,N]; the Collatz peak (e.g. 9232 from 27) and the inverse peak both exceed N for
  many n <= N (the outsiders listed), and each fixed n is inside for all large N.

==============================================================================
S4  Terras-type density theorem for the greedy inverse strategy
==============================================================================
  J=1: mod 3^2 determines the word; mod 3^1 does not, witness (1, 4, [2], [0])
  J=2: mod 3^3 determines the word; mod 3^2 does not, witness (1, 10, [2, 2], [2, 0])
  J=3: mod 3^4 determines the word; mod 3^3 does not, witness (1, 28, [2, 2, 2], [2, 2, 0])
PROVED (S4.1): the first J greedy letters (k_1..k_J) are a function of m mod 3^(J+1); this
  modulus is sharp (witnesses above).

   J | residues | f_J = P(some prefix i<=J has 2^K_i < 3^i) | g_J = P(2^K_J < 3^J) | 1-f_J <= (7/9)^(J-1)? | E[2^K_J] | 3(7/3)^(J-1)
   1 |        6 | 2/3 = 0.666667 | 2/3 = 0.666667 | True | 3 | 3
      all 4 observed 1-letter words have count = 2*3^J * P_chain(word)  (checked)
   2 |       18 | 8/9 = 0.888889 | 5/6 = 0.833333 | True | 7 | 7
      all 12 observed 2-letter words have count = 2*3^J * P_chain(word)  (checked)
   3 |       54 | 25/27 = 0.925926 | 43/54 = 0.796296 | True | 49/3 | 49/3
      all 36 observed 3-letter words have count = 2*3^J * P_chain(word)  (checked)
   4 |      162 | 26/27 = 0.962963 | 73/81 = 0.901235 | True | 343/9 | 343/9
      all 108 observed 4-letter words have count = 2*3^J * P_chain(word)  (checked)
   5 |      486 | 236/243 = 0.971193 | 214/243 = 0.880658 | True | 2401/27 | 2401/27
   6 |     1458 | 239/243 = 0.983539 | 686/729 = 0.941015 | True | 16807/81 | 16807/81
   7 |     4374 | 241/243 = 0.991770 | 2126/2187 = 0.972108 | True | 117649/243 | 117649/243
   8 |    13122 | 2173/2187 = 0.993599 | 12647/13122 = 0.963801 | True | 823543/729 | 823543/729
   9 |    39366 | 19609/19683 = 0.996240 | 19339/19683 = 0.982523 | True | 5764801/2187 | 5764801/2187
  10 |   118098 | 58868/59049 = 0.996935 | 6413/6561 = 0.977442 | True | 40353607/6561 | 40353607/6561
FINITE-EXACT (S4.2): f_J increases toward 1; E[2^K_J] = 3(7/3)^(J-1) exactly for J<=10.
PROVED+checked (S4.3): after one greedy step the residue mod 9 has the exact law
  P(1)=P(4)=P(7)=2/9, P(2)=P(5)=P(8)=1/9 (uniform on units mod 3^6, steps 1..4 checked);
  E_pi[k] = 2*2/9 + 1*1/9 + 0 + 3*1/9 + 0 + 1*1/9 = 1 < log2(3).
FINITE-EXACT (S4.4): for J=6, m<=10^5, 'descends within 6 greedy steps' differs from the pure
  residue prediction only where the carry helps small m: 0 mismatches, all with actual descent
  and predicted growth; largest mismatching m = None
  (prediction => descent is unconditional since the carry B_i > 0).

THEOREM (S4.5, PROVED below in the note): the greedy inverse stopping time
  sigma(m) = min{i : m_i < m} is finite on a set of natural density 1 among the
  non-multiples of 3, with #{m<=X, 3 not|m, sigma(m) > J} <= (7/9)^(J-1) (2X/3) + C_J.
  Mechanism: E[2^{K_J}] = 3 (7/3)^{J-1} (tilted 2x2 matrix [[5/3,1/3],[10/3,2/3]] of rank 1,
  eigenvalue 7/3) + Markov inequality P(2^{K_J} >= 3^J) <= 3(7/3)^{J-1}/3^J = (7/9)^{J-1}.
  checked: det M = 0, tr M = 7/3, (5/2,1/2) M 1 = 7; E[2^{k_1}] = 3.

