PROVED (S8.7): for 2 a primitive root mod p with e != 0, sum_s 2^k0(s) = 2^(p-1)-1, hence
  rho_p = (2^(p-1)-1+2^(p-1+k0(e)))/p >= (2^p-1)/p, which exceeds p for every p >= 5 (2^p-1 > p^2);
  E_pi[k] = (p-1)/2 + k0(e)/p >= (p-1)/2 >= log2 p for every p >= 7 (2^(p-1) >= p^2).  So among ALL such
  primes: mean-subcritical <=> p = 3; log-subcritical <=> p in {3,5}.  For p = 7, 17 (2 not primitive) the
  class law (1+[c=e])/p still holds on every <2>-coset, and the per-step probability of leaving <2>
  (no inverse move at all) is exactly (p-1-|<2>|+[e not in <2>])/p = 3/7 and 8/17.
  p=5 Chernoff certificate: rho(21/20) = 17876501/16000000, rho^10 = 3.031275 < (21/20)^23 = 3.071524, 2^23 < 5^10: rate per step 0.99868.
  E[lambda^K_J] = (1/4) 1^T M^J 1 for p=5, J<=3, lambda in {2, 21/20}: exact.
  E_5 greedy cycles {13,83,33}, {17,27,43} = reversed deterministic 5n+1 cycles; nodes reachable from 1 in
  E_7 within [1,10^4] have residues {1,2,4} mod 7 only (m = 3 refutes the Q2 analogue for p = 7).
  time 0.0s
S8.8 accelerated T-orbit of 31 = 2^5-1 (L=4): [31, 47, 71, 107, 161], ending at 2*3^4-1 = 161 (inherited (B5) block);
  the E-arrow 1 -> 4 is the summand arrow n -> n+(2n+1) with distinct parents (1,3); the diagonal (1,1) -> 2
  of the inherited note is the shortcut arrow 1 -> 2, which is not an E-arrow.

